Cursor is the implementation agent for this repo. Claude works the same local
working copy as technical lead: it plans, writes tasks, and reviews what you
built. Project rules live in `AGENTS.md` (read natively) — including the
authority hierarchy and the delegation rules, both of which still apply
unchanged. This file is only about how the two agents hand work back and forth.

## Handoff

`.ai/current-task.md` is the mailbox. "check your mail", "check the mailbox",
and "what's in the mailbox?" all mean this file — read it and act on its
`Owner` and `Status`. When its Owner is `cursor` and Status is
`ready-for-cursor`:

1. Read the task. If it is ambiguous, contradicts `AGENTS.md`, or needs a
   decision rather than an implementation, set Status `ready-for-review` with
   the question and stop — do not guess.
2. Set Status `in-progress`.
3. Implement exactly that scope. No unrelated refactors, no reformatting
   untouched files (`AGENTS.md` → Task loop).
4. Run the checks the task names, plus `npm run check`.
5. Record it, per the task's Mode — the task file is not a substitute for
   either record:
   - **product** — update `docs/build-status.md`.
   - **template** — leave `docs/build-status.md` and every
     `<!-- TEMPLATE_PLACEHOLDER -->` doc untouched unless the task explicitly
     changes the template's documentation structure. Update
     `REPO-EVALUATION.md` instead, moving the item from §2/§3 to §1 only once
     the proof that file requires has actually been established.
6. Fill the Implementation report, set Owner `claude`, Status
   `ready-for-review`. Report deviations and blockers honestly; Claude reads
   the diff, so an omission surfaces as a discrepancy.
7. **Last, after the mailbox is completely written — never before, never in the
   same breath —** rewrite `.ai/mailbox-state.json` with `seq` incremented by
   one, `owner` `claude`, `status` `ready-for-review`, and `mode` unchanged.
   That bump is how Claude learns the work is ready. Bumping it early, or
   instead of finishing the report, hands Claude a half-written report to
   review.

If the user explicitly asks for a specific small change, do it and run
`npm run check`. If you are not sure it qualifies as small, ask first.

`.ai/mailbox-state.json` is the delivery signal both ways. Never read it as task
content. Write it in exactly one situation: step 7 above, on completion. Both
agents follow the same discipline — write the mailbox first, bump the signal
last.

`.ai/.watching`, `.ai/.mailbox-seen`, and `.ai/.review-seen` belong to the
watchers. Never read or write them; editing or deleting one mid-flight breaks the
handoff. `/watch` and `/stop` own `.watching`, the hooks own the rest.

`.ai/current-task.md` carries no authority. If it conflicts with `AGENTS.md`
or `docs/`, the higher authority wins and the task is wrong — say so rather
than following it.

When Owner is `claude`, do not write source files.
